    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Provider ;
    rdfs:label 'Provider' ;
    rdfs:comment 'Master reference table for provider. ' .

ind:CohortMembership rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Care ;
    rdfs:label 'Cohort Membership' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the enrollment of a member into a population health cohort for targeted care management programs. It captures the member\'s entry into and exit from cohorts, their risk stratification at the time of enrollment, and the current status of their participation. Each record links one member_profile to one cohort with temporal and clinical attributes that exist only in the context of this membership relationship.. Existence Justification: In UnitedHealthcare\'s population health management operations, members are routinely assigned to multiple cohorts simultaneously based on their clinical conditions, risk profiles, and program eligibility (e.g., a member can be in both a \'diabetic\' cohort and a \'high-risk\' cohort at the same time). Each cohort contains thousands of members who meet the defined inclusion criteria. The business actively manages these memberships through care management teams who track when members enter cohorts, their risk scores at entry, enrollment reasons, and current participation status for program effectiveness reporting and targeted interventions.' .

ind:GroupAgreement rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Member ;
    rdfs:label 'Group Agreement' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the Contract between member_group and agreement. It captures the specific terms, rates, and enrollment parameters that apply when a particular employer group is covered under a provider or payer agreement. Each record links one member_group to one agreement with attributes that exist only in the context of this relationship, such as group-specific rate tiers, contribution amounts, and effective dates.. Existence Justification: In health insurance operations, employer groups (member_groups) can be covered under multiple provider or payer agreements simultaneously or over time (e.g., different agreements for different service categories, geographic regions, or renewal periods). Conversely, a single master agreement (especially association plans, multi-employer trusts, or network agreements) can cover multiple employer groups with group-specific rate tiers, contribution levels, and effective dates. The business actively manages these group-agreement relationships as distinct operational entities with their own lifecycle, rates, and terms.' .

ind:NetworkParticipation rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Provider ;
    rdfs:label 'Network Participation' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the participation contract between a healthcare provider and a health insurance product plan. It captures the terms, conditions, and status of each provider\'s participation in each plan\'s network. Each record links one provider to one plan with attributes that define the specific participation arrangement including network tier placement, reimbursement terms, effective dates, and acceptance status.. Existence Justification: In health insurance operations, providers participate in multiple plan networks simultaneously (a cardiologist may be in-network for Medicare Advantage, Commercial PPO, and Medicaid plans), and each plan has thousands of participating providers. Network participation is an actively managed operational entity with specific contract terms, tier assignments, reimbursement schedules, and acceptance statuses that vary by provider-plan combination. Claims adjudication, member cost-sharing calculations, and provider directory services all depend on this participation relationship.' .

ind:ProgramEnrollment rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Member ;
    rdfs:label 'Program Enrollment' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the enrollment relationship between a member and a care or wellness program. It captures the member\'s participation lifecycle including enrollment dates, participation status, assigned care coordinators, and outcome metrics. Each record links one member to one program with attributes that exist only in the context of this enrollment relationship, supporting care management operations, quality reporting (HEDIS/Stars), and program effectiveness analysis.. Existence Justification: In health insurance payer operations, members routinely enroll in multiple concurrent programs (e.g., a diabetic member may be enrolled in Diabetes Management, Wellness Coaching, and Medication Adherence programs simultaneously), and each program serves thousands of members. Care management teams actively manage these enrollments as operational entities, tracking enrollment dates, participation status, assigned care coordinators, and outcome metrics for each member-program combination to support quality reporting (HEDIS/Stars) and program effectiveness analysis.' .

ind:MeasureRiskStratification rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Quality ;
    rdfs:label 'Measure Risk Stratification' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the risk stratification configuration between quality measures and HCC categories. It captures the risk adjustment methodology used to ensure fair performance comparison across member populations with different health risk profiles. Each record links one quality measure to one HCC category with stratification rules and adjustment factors that exist only in the context of this relationship.. Existence Justification: In health insurance quality measurement, quality measures (HEDIS, Star Ratings) must be risk-stratified across multiple HCC categories to ensure fair performance comparison between member populations with different health risk profiles. A single quality measure like \'Controlling High Blood Pressure\' applies to multiple HCC categories (diabetes, cardiovascular, renal), and each HCC category is relevant to multiple quality measures. The business actively manages these stratification configurations with specific adjustment factors, weights, and stratification levels for each measure-HCC pairing.' .

ind:MemberImprovementEnrollment rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Quality ;
    rdfs:label 'Member Improvement Enrollment' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the enrollment of high-risk members into quality improvement initiatives. It captures the operational relationship between improvement plans and member risk profiles, tracking enrollment status, priority, assigned care managers, and target completion dates for each member-plan combination. Each record links one improvement_plan to one member_risk_profile with attributes that exist only in the context of this enrollment relationship.. Existence Justification: In health insurance payer operations, quality improvement plans (such as care gap closure initiatives, chronic disease management programs, and HEDIS measure improvement campaigns) enroll multiple high-risk members simultaneously based on risk stratification and quality measure targeting. Conversely, high-risk members are frequently enrolled in multiple concurrent improvement initiatives addressing different quality domains (e.g., a diabetic member may be enrolled in both a diabetes management plan and a preventive care gap closure plan). Care management teams actively manage these enrollments as operational entities, tracking enrollment dates, completion status, assigned care managers, and target completion dates for each member-plan combination.' .

ind:SanctionRemediation rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Quality ;
    rdfs:label 'Sanction Remediation' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the remediation relationship between provider sanctions and quality improvement plans. It captures the specific corrective actions, compliance requirements, and monitoring activities mandated for each sanctioned provider. Each record links one provider sanction to one improvement plan with attributes that track remediation progress, compliance deadlines, and completion status.. Existence Justification: In health insurance payer operations, provider sanctions for fraud/abuse violations often mandate multiple corrective actions across different domains (clinical quality, billing practices, documentation standards), requiring multiple improvement plans. Conversely, a single improvement plan (e.g., \'Proper Documentation Standards\') can be mandated as remediation for multiple historical sanctions across different providers or violation types. The business actively manages these remediation assignments with specific compliance deadlines, monitoring status, and completion tracking for each sanction-plan pairing.' .

ind:PlanAgentAssignment rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Quality ;
    rdfs:label 'Plan Agent Assignment' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the Assignment between improvement_plan and agent. It captures the operational assignment of service agents to quality improvement initiatives, tracking their specific roles, training completion, and accountability. Each record links one improvement_plan to one agent with attributes that exist only in the context of this assignment relationship.. Existence Justification: In health insurance quality improvement operations, improvement plans require cross-functional teams of service agents with different skills and roles (e.g., training coordinators, data analysts, member outreach specialists) to implement HEDIS and Stars measure improvements. Simultaneously, service agents participate in multiple concurrent improvement initiatives as their skills are needed across different quality domains. The business actively manages these assignments, tracking each agent\'s role, training completion, and contribution hours for accountability and resource planning.' .

ind:ProviderMeasurePerformance rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Quality ;
    rdfs:label 'Provider Measure Performance' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the performance evaluation between quality measures and credentialed providers. It captures provider-specific performance against HEDIS and CMS quality measures for regulatory reporting, Stars ratings, and network performance management. Each record links one quality measure to one credentialed provider with period-specific performance metrics that exist only in the context of this relationship.. Existence Justification: In health insurance payer operations, quality measures are evaluated across many credentialed providers, and each provider is measured against multiple quality measures. UnitedHealthcare actively manages provider-specific performance records for HEDIS reporting, CMS Stars ratings, and network performance management, tracking numerator/denominator counts, compliance status, and performance tiers for each provider-measure-period combination.' .

ind:MeasureProgramTarget rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Quality ;
    rdfs:label 'Measure Program Target' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the operational tracking relationship between quality measures and care/quality programs. It captures program-specific performance targets, measurement periods, and reporting requirements for each measure-program combination. Each record links one quality measure to one program with attributes that define how that measure is tracked, reported, and targeted within the specific program context.. Existence Justification: In health insurance payer operations, quality measures are tracked across multiple programs (Disease Management, Care Coordination, Medicare Advantage, Medicaid), and each program tracks multiple measures with program-specific targets, priorities, and reporting requirements. For example, the \'Controlling High Blood Pressure\' measure (CBP) may be tracked in a Hypertension Management program with an 85% target and monthly reporting, while also being tracked in a general Medicare Advantage program with a 75% target and quarterly reporting. The business actively manages these measure-program combinations to optimize HEDIS scores, CMS Star Ratings, and quality outcomes.' .

ind:ContractQualityMetric rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Shared ;
    rdfs:label 'Contract Quality Metric' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the quality-based performance arrangement between clinical quality metrics and value-based contracts. It captures the specific performance thresholds, incentive payment structures, and measurement parameters that apply when a particular clinical metric is included in a contract\'s quality incentive program. Each record links one clinical metric to one contract with the contract-specific performance targets and payment terms.. Existence Justification: In value-based care contracting, UnitedHealth Care negotiates quality incentive programs where multiple clinical quality metrics (HbA1c control, readmission rates, etc.) are included in a single provider or broker contract, and each metric appears across multiple contracts with different performance thresholds and payment amounts. The business actively manages these contract-metric arrangements as operational entities, tracking contract-specific performance targets, incentive payments, measurement periods, and payment tiers for each metric-contract combination.' .

ind:PlanCostAllocation rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Quality ;
    rdfs:label 'Plan Cost Allocation' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the budget allocation relationship between quality improvement plans and cost centers. It captures how improvement plan budgets are distributed across multiple organizational cost centers, with each allocation tracking the specific amount, percentage, and status of funding from each cost center to each plan. Each record links one improvement plan to one cost center with allocation-specific attributes that exist only in the context of this funding relationship.. Existence Justification: In health insurance operations, quality improvement plans routinely require budget contributions from multiple cost centers across different departments (clinical operations, IT, care management, analytics). Each cost center tracks its specific allocation amount, percentage, and spending against each plan it funds. Conversely, each cost center typically funds multiple improvement plans simultaneously, creating a genuine many-to-many operational relationship where the allocation itself (with amount, dates, status) is actively managed by finance and quality teams.' .

ind:MeasurePolicyMandate rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Quality ;
    rdfs:label 'Measure Policy Mandate' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the regulatory mandate relationship between quality measures and compliance policies. It captures the specific requirements, thresholds, and enforcement rules that apply when a compliance policy mandates the tracking and reporting of a quality measure. Each record links one measure to one compliance_policy with attributes that define how that measure must be performed to satisfy that specific policy requirement.. Existence Justification: In health insurance operations, a single quality measure (e.g., Controlling High Blood Pressure) is mandated by multiple compliance policies simultaneously - CMS Star Ratings, state-specific HEDIS requirements, and NCQA accreditation standards each require the same measure with different thresholds, reporting frequencies, and penalties. Conversely, a single compliance policy (e.g., CMS Medicare Advantage regulations) mandates dozens of different quality measures. Compliance teams actively manage these mandate relationships, tracking which measures satisfy which regulatory requirements with specific performance thresholds and enforcement rules.' .

ind:ProviderScoreAuditCitation rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Quality ;
    rdfs:label 'Provider Score Audit Citation' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the citation or reference of provider quality scores as evidence within audit findings. It captures the specific provider performance metrics documented by auditors during compliance, network adequacy, or quality of care audits. Each record links one provider score to one audit finding with attributes that exist only in the context of this audit citation relationship.. Existence Justification: In health insurance payer audit operations, auditors actively cite provider quality scores as evidence across multiple audit findings (network adequacy, quality of care, credentialing, Stars rating compliance). A single provider score can be referenced in multiple findings across different audits or within the same audit, and a single audit finding typically documents multiple provider scores as supporting evidence. Auditors create citation records that capture the score value at audit time, assign severity, track remediation deadlines, and manage validation status.' .

ind:MeasureRequirement rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Quality ;
    rdfs:label 'Measure Requirement' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the regulatory requirement between quality measures and government benefit designs. It captures the mandatory reporting obligations, compliance thresholds, and measurement specifications that apply when a specific HEDIS or CMS quality measure is required for a particular government program benefit design. Each record links one measure to one benefit design with attributes that exist only in the context of this regulatory requirement.. Existence Justification: In health insurance payer operations, government programs (Medicare Advantage, Medicaid, Dual Eligible SNPs) each mandate different sets of HEDIS and CMS quality measures with program-specific reporting requirements, compliance thresholds, and measurement frequencies. A single quality measure like \'Controlling High Blood Pressure\' applies to multiple benefit designs (MA PPO, MA HMO, Medicaid Managed Care) with different thresholds and reporting frequencies, while each benefit design must comply with multiple quality measures simultaneously. The business actively manages these regulatory requirements as a compliance function.' .

ind:SharedProgramEnrollment rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Shared ;
    rdfs:label 'Shared Program Enrollment' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the enrollment of a health plan member into a care management or wellness program. It captures the participation relationship between a member\'s health plan enrollment and specific programs (e.g., Diabetes Management, COPD Care, Weight Management). Each record links one enrollment to one program with attributes that track participation status, enrollment timing, completion, and engagement level.. Existence Justification: In health insurance operations, members (via their enrollments) can simultaneously participate in multiple care management and wellness programs (e.g., a diabetic member may be enrolled in Diabetes Management, Weight Management, and Preventive Care programs concurrently). Conversely, each program serves many enrolled members across the health plan. Care management teams actively manage these program enrollments as operational entities, tracking enrollment dates, participation status, completion, and engagement levels for each member-program combination.' .

ind:AuditHccFinding rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Fraud ;
    rdfs:label 'Audit Hcc Finding' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the audit finding relationship between provider audits and individual HCC assignments. It captures the validation results, documentation quality assessment, and error identification for each HCC assignment reviewed during a provider audit. Each record links one provider audit to one member HCC assignment with attributes that exist only in the context of this audit review.. Existence Justification: In health insurance payer operations, provider audits routinely review multiple HCC assignments across many members to validate diagnosis coding accuracy and documentation quality. Individual HCC assignments can be audited multiple times through different audit types (annual compliance audits, targeted reviews, CMS RADV audits). The business actively manages audit findings as operational records with specific validation statuses, documentation quality scores, error types, and financial impacts for each HCC assignment reviewed.' .

ind:AuditInteraction rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Fraud ;
    rdfs:label 'Audit Interaction' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the documented communication events between auditors and providers during the audit lifecycle. It captures the interaction context, audit phase, and documentation exchange that occurs as part of provider audit execution. Each record links one provider_audit to one interaction with attributes that exist only in the context of this relationship.. Existence Justification: In health insurance payer operations, provider audits generate multiple service interactions throughout their lifecycle (initial notification calls, document request emails, clarification discussions, results presentations). A single interaction can reference multiple concurrent audits when a provider is under review for multiple issues simultaneously. The business actively manages these audit-interaction linkages to track communication history, documentation exchange, and audit phase progression.' .

ind:PolicyViolation rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Fraud ;
    rdfs:label 'Policy Violation' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the violation event between investigation and compliance_policy. It captures the specific instance where a fraud investigation identifies a violation of a particular compliance policy. Each record links one investigation to one compliance_policy with attributes that document the violation details, severity, and findings for regulatory reporting and remediation tracking.. Existence Justification: In health insurance fraud investigations, a single investigation routinely identifies violations of multiple compliance policies (HIPAA privacy rules, Anti-Kickback Statute, False Claims Act, billing policies, etc.), and each compliance policy can be violated across many different investigations. The business actively manages policy violations as distinct operational records with specific findings, severity assessments, and regulatory reporting requirements for each investigation-policy combination.' .

ind:InvestigationProgramScope rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Fraud ;
    rdfs:label 'Investigation Program Scope' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the investigative scope between fraud investigations and provider program participations. It captures the specific government programs under investigation, program-specific violations discovered, investigation status per program, and assigned investigators. Each record links one investigation to one provider program participation with attributes that exist only in the context of this relationship.. Existence Justification: In health insurance payer fraud operations, a single investigation routinely examines multiple government program participations when providers commit cross-program fraud (e.g., billing fraud across Medicare Advantage, Medicaid, and CHIP simultaneously). Conversely, a single provider program participation can be subject to multiple concurrent or sequential investigations for different fraud types (billing irregularities, kickback schemes, quality violations). SIU investigators actively manage investigation scope records per program, tracking program-specific violations, findings, status, and assigned investigators.' .

ind:InvestigationAudit rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Fraud ;
    rdfs:label 'Investigation Audit' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the audit event between investigation and audit. It captures the formal review and examination of fraud investigation cases by internal compliance teams, external auditors, or regulatory bodies. Each record links one investigation to one audit engagement with attributes that exist only in the context of this specific audit review, including the scope of review, findings specific to that investigation, and the auditor assigned to review that particular case.. Existence Justification: In health insurance payer operations, fraud investigations are subject to multiple types of audits throughout their lifecycle: internal quality assurance reviews, compliance audits, regulatory audits (CMS, state insurance departments), and legal sufficiency reviews. Conversely, a single audit engagement (e.g., annual SIU compliance audit) examines a sample of multiple investigation cases to assess overall program effectiveness, adherence to protocols, and regulatory compliance. The business actively manages this relationship by tracking which investigations were audited, what findings were identified per investigation, and what remediation is required.' .

ind:ContractAudit rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Shared ;
    rdfs:label 'Contract Audit' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the audit review relationship between provider audits and contracts. It captures which specific contracts were reviewed during a provider audit, the scope of review for each contract, the compliance findings, and the specific contract clauses examined. Each record links one provider audit to one contract with attributes that exist only in the context of this audit-contract relationship.. Existence Justification: In health insurance payer operations, provider audits routinely review multiple contracts simultaneously because providers typically have multiple contractual agreements with UnitedHealth Care (e.g., professional services contract, facility contract, ancillary services contract, different lines of business). Conversely, each contract undergoes multiple audits over its lifecycle (annual compliance audits, triggered audits based on billing anomalies, random sample audits, renewal audits). Network operations and fraud teams actively manage the audit-contract relationship by tracking which specific contract terms were reviewed, the compliance status per contract, and the audit findings attributable to each contract.' .

ind:BrokerProgramAppointment rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Sales ;
    rdfs:label 'Broker Program Appointment' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the appointment relationship between insurance brokers and government program participation contracts. It captures broker certifications, appointment dates, territory assignments, and commission structures specific to each broker-program combination. Each record links one broker to one provider program participation with attributes that exist only in the context of this appointment relationship.. Existence Justification: In the health insurance payer industry, brokers are appointed to sell multiple government programs (Medicare Advantage, Part D, Medicaid), and each government program has multiple certified brokers selling it across different territories. UnitedHealthcare actively manages these broker appointments as a core business process, tracking certifications, territories, commission structures, and appointment statuses specific to each broker-program combination.' .

ind:CampaignEnrollment rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Marketing ;
    rdfs:label 'Campaign Enrollment' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the enrollment of a member risk profile into a marketing campaign. It captures the operational relationship between risk-stratified members and targeted campaigns, tracking when each member was enrolled, their response to campaign outreach, eligibility reasoning, and priority scoring for outreach sequencing. Each record links one member risk profile to one campaign with attributes that exist only in the context of this enrollment relationship.. Existence Justification: In health insurance payer operations, member risk profiles are actively enrolled into multiple marketing campaigns simultaneously (e.g., care gap closure, wellness programs, disease management outreach), and each campaign targets multiple member risk profiles based on risk stratification criteria. Campaign enrollment is an operational business process managed by marketing teams who create enrollment records, track member responses, prioritize outreach sequences, and document eligibility reasoning for each member-campaign combination.' .

ind:BrokerCampaignAssignment rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Sales ;
    rdfs:label 'Broker Campaign Assignment' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the Assignment between broker and campaign. It captures the operational assignment of insurance brokers to specific marketing campaigns with performance targets, territory coverage, and commission structures. Each record links one broker to one campaign with attributes that exist only in the context of this relationship.. Existence Justification: In UnitedHealthcare\'s operations, insurance brokers are assigned to participate in multiple marketing campaigns simultaneously (Medicare campaigns, employer group campaigns, individual marketplace campaigns), and each campaign engages multiple brokers across different territories and market segments. The business actively manages these assignments with broker-specific performance targets, territory coverage, commission rates, and performance tiers that vary by broker-campaign combination. This is an operational assignment process, not an analytical correlation.' .

ind:ChannelCampaignExecution rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Sales ;
    rdfs:label 'Channel Campaign Execution' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the operational execution of a marketing campaign through a specific sales channel. It captures the budget allocation, performance targets, and execution parameters that exist only when a campaign is deployed through a particular channel. Each record links one campaign to one sales channel with channel-specific execution details including budget, lead volume targets, priority, and active dates.. Existence Justification: In health insurance marketing operations, campaigns are routinely executed across multiple sales channels simultaneously (e.g., a Medicare AEP campaign runs through broker, direct enrollment, and call center channels concurrently), and each sales channel supports multiple campaigns throughout the year (e.g., the Broker Direct channel may run Commercial, Medicare, and Marketplace campaigns). Marketing teams actively manage channel-campaign execution as a distinct operational entity, allocating channel-specific budgets, setting channel-specific lead targets, and tracking performance by channel-campaign combination.' .

ind:ProgramDiagnosisMapping rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Shared ;
    rdfs:label 'Program Diagnosis Mapping' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the configuration mapping between diagnosis codes and quality/care programs. It captures the business rules that define which diagnosis codes are tracked by which programs, along with program-specific weights, reporting requirements, and quality measure indicators. Each record links one diagnosis code to one program with attributes that define how that diagnosis is evaluated within the program context.. Existence Justification: In health insurance operations, diagnosis codes are mapped to multiple quality and care management programs simultaneously (e.g., a diabetes diagnosis code impacts HEDIS diabetes measures, Star Ratings, and disease management programs), and each program tracks multiple diagnosis codes with program-specific weights and reporting rules. This is a configuration-driven operational relationship where the business actively manages which diagnoses are tracked by which programs, with relationship-specific attributes like program_weight, reporting_requirement, and quality_measure_indicator that vary by the diagnosis-program combination.' .

ind:CampaignContent rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Marketing ;
    rdfs:label 'Campaign Content' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the reference relationship between knowledge_base articles and marketing campaigns. It captures which knowledge base articles are made available or referenced for agent training and customer support during specific marketing campaigns. Each record links one knowledge_base article to one campaign with usage metrics that exist only in the context of this relationship.. Existence Justification: In health insurance marketing operations, campaigns require multiple knowledge base articles to support agents handling campaign-specific inquiries (Medicare enrollment campaigns need articles on eligibility, enrollment periods, plan comparisons, etc.), and knowledge base articles are reused across multiple campaigns (an article on \'Medicare Part D enrollment\' supports annual enrollment campaigns, special enrollment campaigns, and supplemental insurance campaigns). The business actively manages which articles are assigned to which campaigns for agent training and tracks usage metrics per campaign-article combination.' .

ind:BrokerProgramParticipation rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Sales ;
    rdfs:label 'Broker Program Participation' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the participation relationship between insurance brokers and  health Care programs. It captures broker enrollment in certification programs, incentive programs, and training initiatives. Each record links one broker to one program with attributes that track certification status, performance metrics, and participation dates specific to that broker-program combination.. Existence Justification: In  health Care\'s broker management operations, brokers participate in multiple programs simultaneously (Medicare Stars certification, wellness initiative training, preferred partner tiers, AEP training programs), and each program has many participating brokers. The business actively manages these participation relationships, tracking certification status, performance tiers, and participation dates for each broker-program combination. This is an operational relationship that broker management teams create, update, and query regularly.' .

ind:CredentialingNetworkParticipation rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Credentialing ;
    rdfs:label 'Credentialing Network Participation' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the participation contract between a credentialed provider and a government benefit design. It captures the terms under which a provider participates in a specific government program\'s network, including tiering, reimbursement modifiers, and participation status. Each record links one credential to one benefit_design with attributes that exist only in the context of this network participation relationship.. Existence Justification: In health insurance payer operations, a credentialed provider participates in multiple government benefit designs (Medicare Advantage plans, Medicaid products, etc.) simultaneously, and each benefit design has hundreds to thousands of participating providers. Network management teams actively manage these participation relationships as distinct operational entities, tracking provider-specific tiering, reimbursement modifiers, and participation status for each credential-benefit design combination. This is a core operational process for provider directory accuracy, claims payment, and member access.' .

ind:PolicyAcknowledgment rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Service ;
    rdfs:label 'Policy Acknowledgment' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the acknowledgment event between agent and compliance_policy. It captures the formal record of when each agent acknowledged each version of each compliance policy, required for regulatory audit trails and compliance tracking. Each record links one agent to one compliance_policy with attributes that exist only in the context of this acknowledgment relationship.. Existence Justification: In health insurance operations, agents must acknowledge multiple compliance policies (HIPAA, Privacy, Fraud Prevention, ACA, state-specific regulations), and each policy requires acknowledgment from all agents who handle member data or perform regulated activities. The business actively manages this relationship through compliance tracking systems that monitor acknowledgment status, send reminders for overdue acknowledgments, and maintain audit trails for regulatory inspections. When policies are updated, all agents must re-acknowledge the new version, creating a historical record of acknowledgments over time.' .

ind:ProviderTrainingCompletion rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Credentialing ;
    rdfs:label 'Provider Training Completion' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the completion event between a provider credential and a compliance training requirement. It captures the fulfillment of mandatory training requirements as part of the credentialing and recredentialing process. Each record links one credential to one training_record with attributes that exist only in the context of this specific completion event.. Existence Justification: In health insurance payer operations, providers must complete multiple mandatory compliance trainings (HIPAA, fraud/waste/abuse, cultural competency, etc.) as part of both initial credentialing and ongoing recredentialing cycles. Each provider credential requires multiple training completions, and the same training module is completed by multiple providers. The credentialing team actively manages these training requirements as a prerequisite for credential approval and tracks completion status, scores, expiration dates, and attestations for each provider-training combination.' .

ind:CredentialPolicyAcknowledgment rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Credentialing ;
    rdfs:label 'Credential Policy Acknowledgment' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the acknowledgment event between a provider credential and a compliance policy. It captures the formal attestation by providers to specific compliance policies during credentialing and recredentialing processes. Each record links one credential to one compliance policy with acknowledgment date, version, status, and signature details that exist only in the context of this relationship.. Existence Justification: In health insurance payer credentialing operations, providers must formally acknowledge multiple compliance policies (Code of Conduct, Privacy Policy, HIPAA, Fraud Prevention, etc.) during initial credentialing and every recredentialing cycle (typically every 36 months). Each provider-policy acknowledgment is tracked as a distinct operational record with acknowledgment date, policy version, signature, and status. Conversely, each compliance policy is acknowledged by thousands of providers across the network, creating a true many-to-many operational relationship.' .

ind:CommitteePolicyGovernance rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Credentialing ;
    rdfs:label 'Committee Policy Governance' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the governance relationship between credentialing committees and compliance policies. It captures which committees have adopted and oversee which compliance policies, including adoption decisions, review schedules, and governance accountability. Each record links one credentialing committee to one compliance policy with attributes that exist only in the context of this governance relationship.. Existence Justification: In health insurance payer operations, credentialing committees are governance bodies that adopt and oversee multiple compliance policies (credentialing standards, peer review protocols, quality thresholds). Each committee-policy relationship represents a formal governance assignment with adoption decisions, review schedules, and accountability tracking. Multiple committees (medical, dental, behavioral health) may govern the same policy from different specialty perspectives, and each committee governs multiple policies within their scope of authority.' .

ind:Certification rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Service ;
    rdfs:label 'Certification' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the certification relationship between service agents and health insurance programs. It captures the training, certification, and authorization status required for agents to service specific programs (Medicare, Medicaid, ACA, Commercial). Each record links one agent to one program with certification dates, specialization levels, and active status that exist only in the context of this agent-program pairing.. Existence Justification: In health insurance payer operations, service agents must be certified to handle specific programs (Medicare, Medicaid, ACA, Commercial) due to regulatory requirements and product complexity. A single agent is typically certified for multiple programs to provide flexible workforce coverage, and each program requires multiple certified agents to handle member inquiries and service requests. The business actively manages these certifications with training dates, specialization levels, expiration tracking, and active status to ensure compliance and proper call routing.' .

ind:ArticleProgramApplicability rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Service ;
    rdfs:label 'Article Program Applicability' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the applicability relationship between knowledge base articles and programs. It captures which support articles, FAQs, and troubleshooting guides are applicable to which health insurance programs (Medicare Advantage, Medicaid, Commercial, etc.), including version control and approval tracking for compliance purposes. Each record links one knowledge base article to one program with effective dates, version tracking, and approval status that exist only in the context of this program-specific applicability.. Existence Justification: In health insurance operations, knowledge base articles (FAQs, troubleshooting guides, policy explanations) must be applicable to multiple programs simultaneously because the same content often applies across Medicare Advantage, Medicaid, and Commercial lines of business. Conversely, each program requires multiple knowledge articles covering different topics (enrollment, benefits, claims, appeals). The business actively manages this relationship for regulatory compliance, requiring program-specific approval, version control, and effective dating because CMS and state regulators require documented evidence that member-facing content is approved for each specific program context.' .

ind:CredentialingProviderProgramParticipation rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Credentialing ;
    rdfs:label 'Credentialing Provider Program Participation' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the participation relationship between a credentialed provider and a health plan program. It captures the operational enrollment and participation status of providers across multiple programs (Medicare Advantage, Medicaid, commercial networks, specialty programs). Each record links one provider credential to one program with enrollment dates, participation status, and tier levels that exist only in the context of this specific provider-program relationship.. Existence Justification: In health insurance payer operations, providers are credentialed to participate in multiple distinct programs simultaneously (Medicare Advantage, Medicaid, commercial PPO/HMO networks, specialty programs like behavioral health or oncology centers of excellence). Each provider-program participation is an operational business entity managed by network operations with its own enrollment date, participation status, tier level, and effective/termination dates. Programs enroll multiple providers with varying participation levels and statuses.' .

ind:ProgramCredentialingPolicy rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Shared ;
    rdfs:label 'Program Credentialing Policy' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the assignment of credentialing policies to specific programs within  health Care. It captures program-specific credentialing requirements and policy variations that exist only in the context of a particular program\'s compliance framework. Each record links one credentialing policy to one program with effective dates and program-specific requirement overrides.. Existence Justification: In  health Care\'s operations, credentialing policies must be applied across multiple programs (Medicare Advantage, Medicaid, Commercial) because each program has distinct regulatory requirements and compliance frameworks. A single base credentialing policy (e.g., primary care physician credentialing) is referenced by multiple programs, but each program applies it with program-specific variations, effective dates, and override requirements. The compliance team actively manages these program-policy assignments as operational records, tracking when policies become effective for each program and what program-specific modifications apply.' .

ind:WorkflowStepSla rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Credentialing ;
    rdfs:label 'Workflow Step Sla' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the SLA assignment between credentialing workflow steps and service level agreements. It captures the specific SLA targets, escalation thresholds, and business priorities that apply when a particular workflow step must meet a particular SLA. Each record links one workflow step to one SLA with attributes that define the operational performance expectations for that specific combination.. Existence Justification: In UnitedHealthcare\'s credentialing operations, workflow steps can be governed by multiple SLAs simultaneously (standard processing, expedited review, regulatory compliance timeframes), and each SLA definition applies to multiple workflow steps across the credentialing process. Operations teams actively configure and manage these assignments, tracking compliance metrics, escalation thresholds, and business priorities specific to each workflow step-SLA combination. This is an operational configuration that credentialing managers create, update, and monitor as part of performance management.' .

ind:CostCenterContractAllocation rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Finance ;
    rdfs:label 'Cost Center Contract Allocation' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the financial allocation relationship between cost centers and contracts. It captures how contract costs are distributed across multiple organizational cost centers for P&L reporting and financial performance tracking. Each record links one cost center to one contract with allocation percentages and methods that exist only in the context of this financial distribution.. Existence Justification: In health insurance payer operations, provider and vendor contracts frequently span multiple organizational cost centers (regions, lines of business, departments) for financial reporting purposes. Finance teams actively manage allocation rules that distribute contract costs across cost centers using specific percentages and methods. This is an operational financial management process where allocation records are created, updated, and tracked over time with specific effective dates and allocation methods.' .

ind:BudgetAllocation rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Finance ;
    rdfs:label 'Budget Allocation' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the allocation of budget amounts to specific programs. It captures the financial planning and tracking relationship between budgets and programs, recording how much of each budget is allocated to each program, when the allocation was made, and its approval status. Each record links one budget to one program with attributes that exist only in the context of this allocation relationship.. Existence Justification: In health insurance operations, a single budget line (e.g., Medical Management Operating Budget) is routinely allocated across multiple programs (Diabetes Management, COPD Care, Preventive Wellness), and conversely, a single program typically receives funding from multiple budget sources (operating budget, medical expense budget, administrative budget, grant funding). Finance actively manages these allocations as discrete records with specific amounts, approval workflows, and fiscal year tracking.' .

ind:ContractJournalAllocation rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Shared ;
    rdfs:label 'Contract Journal Allocation' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the allocation relationship between journal entries and contracts. It captures how financial journal entries are attributed to specific provider or broker contracts for profitability analysis, cost tracking, and audit trails. Each record links one journal entry to one contract with allocation details that exist only in the context of this relationship.. Existence Justification: In health insurance payer operations, large provider and broker contracts generate multiple journal entries across accounting periods for various financial events (capitation payments, risk settlements, quality incentives, commissions). Finance teams actively manage the allocation of journal entries to contracts for contract profitability analysis, cost tracking, and audit compliance. A single journal entry can be split across multiple contracts (e.g., shared administrative costs), and each contract accumulates many journal entries over its lifecycle.' .

ind:CampaignBenefitPromotion rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Marketing ;
    rdfs:label 'Campaign Benefit Promotion' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the promotional relationship between marketing campaigns and government benefit designs. It captures which benefit plans are featured in which marketing campaigns during specific enrollment periods, with targeted enrollment goals and campaign priority. Each record links one campaign to one benefit_design with attributes that exist only in the context of this promotional relationship.. Existence Justification: In health insurance marketing operations, a single marketing campaign routinely promotes multiple benefit designs simultaneously (e.g., a Medicare Annual Enrollment Period campaign features PPO, HMO, and SNP plan options across different regions). Conversely, each benefit design appears in multiple campaigns throughout the year (AEP campaigns, Special Enrollment Period campaigns, year-round acquisition campaigns). Marketing teams actively manage these promotional relationships by setting enrollment targets, priority levels, and tracking which plans are featured in which campaigns during specific enrollment periods.' .

ind:AssetComplianceReview rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Compliance ;
    rdfs:label 'Asset Compliance Review' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the compliance review relationship between compliance policies and marketing assets. It captures the regulatory review and approval process required before marketing materials can be used in campaigns. Each record links one compliance policy to one marketing asset with review tracking attributes that exist only in the context of this regulatory assessment.. Existence Justification: In health insurance payer operations, marketing assets must undergo compliance review against multiple regulatory policies (HIPAA privacy rules, CMS marketing guidelines, state-specific insurance advertising regulations, ADA accessibility standards). Each asset-policy combination requires independent review tracking because a single asset may be compliant with HIPAA but non-compliant with a specific state regulation, or may receive an exception for one policy but not another. The compliance team actively manages these reviews as operational records with review dates, reviewer assignments, approval statuses, and exception grants.' .

ind:BenefitComplianceRequirement rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Compliance ;
    rdfs:label 'Benefit Compliance Requirement' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the regulatory compliance requirements that apply to government benefit designs. It captures the specific compliance obligations, attestation status, and review cycles for each benefit design-policy pairing. Each record links one compliance policy to one benefit design with attributes that track compliance status, effective dates, and requirement types that exist only in the context of this regulatory relationship.. Existence Justification: In health insurance payer operations, government benefit designs (Medicare Advantage, Medicaid, Part D plans) must comply with multiple regulatory policies simultaneously—CMS coverage mandates, formulary requirements, network adequacy standards, prior authorization protocols, and cost-sharing limits. Each benefit design is subject to multiple compliance policies, and each compliance policy applies across multiple benefit designs spanning different programs, plan years, and service areas. Compliance teams actively manage these mappings, tracking attestation status, review cycles, and audit findings for each benefit-policy pairing.' .

ind:CampaignSurveyDistribution rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Marketing ;
    rdfs:label 'Campaign Survey Distribution' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the distribution event between campaign and survey. It captures the operational deployment of specific surveys within marketing campaigns to targeted member populations. Each record links one campaign to one survey with attributes that track distribution timing, response metrics, and audience targeting that exist only in the context of this specific campaign-survey deployment.. Existence Justification: In health insurance marketing operations, campaigns routinely distribute multiple survey types (CAHPS satisfaction surveys, needs assessments, enrollment intent surveys) to measure different aspects of campaign effectiveness and member engagement. Conversely, standardized survey instruments (especially regulatory-required CAHPS surveys) are reused across multiple campaigns targeting different member segments, time periods, and lines of business. Marketing teams actively manage these campaign-survey distributions as operational records, tracking distribution dates, response rates, target audience sizes, and survey purposes specific to each campaign-survey pairing.' .

ind:OfferEligibilityRule rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Marketing ;
    rdfs:label 'Offer Eligibility Rule' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the configuration relationship between marketing offers and eligibility rules. It captures which eligibility rules are applied to which offers, with context-specific metadata about how and when each rule is applied to determine member qualification for promotional offers. Each record links one offer to one eligibility rule with attributes that govern the rule\'s application in the context of that specific offer.. Existence Justification: In health insurance marketing operations, offers require complex eligibility determination through multiple rules (age, geography, income, medical conditions), and each eligibility rule is reusable across multiple offers. Product teams actively configure which rules apply to which offers, setting offer-specific priorities, effective dates, and application contexts. This is an operational configuration relationship that the business actively manages, not an analytical correlation.' .

ind:BenefitEligibilityRule rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Government ;
    rdfs:label 'Benefit Eligibility Rule' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the configuration relationship between benefit designs and eligibility rules in government health insurance programs. It captures which eligibility rules are applied to each benefit design, with rule-specific precedence, effective dates, and override capabilities. Each record links one benefit design to one eligibility rule with attributes that govern how that rule is evaluated for that specific benefit plan.. Existence Justification: In government health insurance programs, a single benefit design (e.g., Medicare Advantage plan) must apply multiple eligibility rules simultaneously (age 65+, ESRD eligibility, disability status, residency requirements, SNP criteria). Conversely, the same eligibility rule (e.g., age 65+ for Medicare) is reused across hundreds of different benefit designs. Product configuration teams actively manage these rule assignments with design-specific effective dates, rule precedence, and override flags.' .

ind:ContractSla rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Government ;
    rdfs:label 'Contract Sla' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the Service Level Agreement commitment between a government program provider contract and a specific SLA definition. It captures the contractual performance obligations, measurement periods, compliance tracking, and penalty enforcement for each contract-SLA combination. Each record links one program_provider_contract to one sla with attributes that exist only in the context of this contractual commitment.. Existence Justification: In health insurance payer operations, government program provider contracts (Medicare Advantage, Medicaid managed care) include multiple distinct SLA obligations covering different service areas (claims processing turnaround, prior authorization response time, member access standards, grievance resolution, provider directory accuracy). Each SLA is a standardized template that applies to multiple contracts across the provider network. Network operations and contract management teams actively track compliance, measure performance, assess penalties, and report on each contract-SLA combination as a managed business entity.' .

ind:ProgramCompliancePolicy rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Shared ;
    rdfs:label 'Program Compliance Policy' ;
    rdfs:comment 'This association product represents the application of compliance policies to specific health insurance programs. It captures program-specific compliance requirements, implementation timelines, and attestation rules. Each record links one compliance policy to one program with attributes that reflect how that policy is implemented and enforced within the context of that specific program.. Existence Justification: In health insurance operations, compliance policies must be applied across multiple programs (Medicare Advantage, Medicaid, Commercial), and each program is subject to multiple compliance policies. The relationship is actively managed by compliance teams who track program-specific implementation dates, scope variations, attestation requirements, and review frequencies. This is an operational relationship where the business creates, updates, and monitors policy-program associations with relationship-specific data.' .

ind:Verification rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Eligibility ;
    rdfs:label 'Verification' ;
    rdfs:comment 'Real-time eligibility verification request and response records capturing member ID, provider ID, service date, eligibility outcome, and comprehensive benefit determination results including coverage levels, deductibles, out-of-pocket maximums, copays, coinsurance, prior authorization requirements, and coordination of benefits information.' .

ind:ProductPlan rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Product ;
    rdfs:label 'Product Plan' ;
    rdfs:comment 'Master record for each health insurance product offering, including plan name, type (HMO, PPO, HDHP), market segment, benefit design, cost-sharing structure, eligibility rules, pricing tiers, effective dates, and high-level attributes. Serves as the comprehensive catalog of health plan offerings across all lines of business.' .

ind:GeneralLedger rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Finance ;
    rdfs:label 'General Ledger' ;
    rdfs:comment 'Authoritative collection of all posted journal entries organized by account and period, serving as the source for financial reporting. Each entry is a transactional record of a financial event, containing debits, credits, posting date, period, and supporting documentation.' .

ind:Commission rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Sales ;
    rdfs:label 'Commission' ;
    rdfs:comment 'Comprehensive record of commission transactions and payments for brokers and sales agents, including commission calculations, earnings, payment processing, reconciliation, and adjustments. Captures the full lifecycle from commission accrual through payment disbursement, including rates, amounts, payment methods, tax withholding, chargebacks, bonuses, overrides, and compliance tracking. Links to policies, members, groups, and sales transactions.' .

ind:TeamMember rdf:type rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ind:Care ;
    rdfs:label 'Team Member' ;
    rdfs:comment 'Master record linking clinical staff or providers to a care team, with role, start/end dates, and credential references.' .

